+/* The 0c6e is a 150x52 strip (7.5 x 2.6 mm at 508 dpi): 2.6 mm is far too
+ * short to press a fingertip against, so the only way to cover a finger is a
+ * swipe, and a swipe needs enough frames to cover it.
+ *
+ * Frame rate: this device sits on a USB full-speed bus (measured: the sysfs
+ * "speed" attribute of its USB device reads 12) with 64-byte bulk endpoints.
+ * Full-speed
+ * bulk tops out at 19 x 64 B per 1 ms frame = 1216 B/ms, so the 15600-byte
+ * image alone takes >= 12.8 ms, and with the pre-scan OUT+IN and the
+ * get_image OUT issued as separate sequential URBs a frame costs roughly
+ * 16-20 ms, i.e. 50-60 fps.
+ *
+ * A deliberate swipe over a ~15 mm fingertip at 15-25 mm/s takes 0.6-1.0 s,
+ * which is 30-60 frames, and a slow or hesitant one is longer. The stock cap
+ * of 30 is therefore 0.5-0.6 s of contact: it truncates a real swipe part
+ * way through and throws away its tail. It also caps the assembled length at
+ * (30 - 1 - ELAN_SKIP_LAST_FRAMES) * delta_y + frame_height.
+ *
+ * 100 frames is 1.7-2.0 s of contact, longer than any deliberate swipe, so
+ * the terminator becomes the 200 ms finger-lift timeout (the intended one)
+ * rather than the cap. The cost is bounded and small: 100 raw frames is
+ * 100 * 150 * 52 * 2 = 1.5 MB, the processed copies are another 0.75 MB, and
+ * movement estimation is O(frames). Left at 30 for every other device, none
+ * of which is this thin. */
